1. Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design|nist_cloud|A team provisions compute automatically without a provider employee fulfilling each request. Which cloud characteristic is illustrated?|On-demand self-service|Manual capacity reservation|Single tenancy|Physical media sanitization|On-demand self-service lets consumers provision resources without human interaction with the provider for each request.
1. Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design|nist_cloud|A service adds capacity rapidly during demand spikes and releases it afterward. Which characteristic fits?|Rapid elasticity|Data sovereignty|Non-repudiation|Cryptographic erase|Elasticity concerns resource expansion and contraction as demand changes.
1. Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design|shared|In a typical IaaS deployment, who patches the customer's guest operating system?|The customer, unless a separate managed-service agreement assigns the task|The hardware manufacturer in every case|The SaaS application's end users|The cloud provider automatically in every IaaS service|IaaS customers generally manage guest operating systems; responsibilities must be checked against the actual service and contract.
1. Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design|shared|Which responsibility generally remains with a SaaS customer?|Managing its data, identities, and permitted access|Maintaining the provider's physical data center|Patching the provider's hypervisor|Replacing failed provider disks|Outsourcing infrastructure does not remove the customer's data and access responsibilities.
1. Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design|nist_cloud|A developer deploys application code while the provider operates the underlying OS and runtime platform. Which service model fits best?|PaaS|IaaS with customer-managed OS|On-premises colocation|Bare-metal hosting with customer-managed runtime|PaaS provides an application platform while the customer manages its deployed application and associated data.
1. Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design|nist_cloud|Which deployment model combines distinct private and public clouds with mechanisms connecting them?|Hybrid cloud|Private cloud only|Community cloud only|A single public-cloud account|Hybrid combines distinct cloud infrastructures while enabling integration between them.
1. Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design|nist_privacy|Before migrating a regulated workload, what should drive cloud control selection?|Business requirements, data sensitivity, threats, and applicable obligations|The provider with the lowest storage price alone|The largest advertised virtual machine alone|The interface with the fewest configuration options alone|A defensible cloud design begins with the organization's workload, security, privacy, and risk requirements.
1. Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design|nist_privacy|A company may need to leave its provider in two years. Which design consideration addresses this risk?|Portability, export formats, and tested exit procedures|Increasing proprietary dependencies without documentation|Assuming provider bankruptcy cannot happen|Replacing backup testing with an uptime dashboard|Exit capability and portability reduce lock-in risk and support continuity during provider changes.
1. Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design|zero|An internal workload requests a sensitive service. What aligns with zero trust?|Authenticate and authorize the request rather than trusting network location alone|Grant access because both systems are on the same subnet|Treat every private IP address as an administrator|Remove access checks after the first successful login|Zero trust does not grant implicit trust solely from network placement or ownership.
1. Cloud Concepts, Architecture and Design|outline|A provider supplies an AI service. Which responsibility must a customer still evaluate?|Its training or input data, model configuration, and allowed use of outputs|Only the provider's office access badges|Only the color of the model dashboard|No customer responsibility after procurement|AI workloads extend shared-responsibility analysis to customer-controlled data and application behavior.
2. Cloud Data Security|outline|Sensitive data is about to enter a new cloud analytics pipeline. What is the best first information-governance step?|Discover and classify the data and identify its flows|Encrypt every field with an undocumented custom algorithm|Delete the retention policy|Assume all data has the same sensitivity|Discovery, classification, and flow understanding inform appropriate controls across the data lifecycle.
2. Cloud Data Security|crypto|Which statement best distinguishes encryption from hashing?|Encryption can be reversed with appropriate keys; a cryptographic hash is not intended to be reversed|Hashing is a form of reversible encryption|Encryption and hashing are interchangeable access-control systems|Hashing always conceals short predictable values from guessing|Encryption protects confidentiality; hashes support integrity checks but predictable inputs may still be guessed.
2. Cloud Data Security|secrets|An application stores its data-encryption key beside its encrypted data under the same broad access permissions. What is the main concern?|One compromise could expose both ciphertext and the key|The encrypted data becomes impossible to back up|The key automatically becomes longer|The data loses all availability guarantees immediately|Key separation and restricted key access reduce the chance that a data-store compromise also exposes decryption capability.
2. Cloud Data Security|outline|A payment system replaces sensitive account values with surrogate values linked through a protected vault. Which technique fits?|Tokenization|Hashing every database table name|Compression|Network address translation|Tokenization substitutes surrogate values while protecting the mapping to the originals.
2. Cloud Data Security|outline|A team wants to detect sensitive files being sent to unauthorized external destinations. Which control is most directly relevant?|Data loss prevention|A CPU capacity dashboard|A DNS availability check alone|A software licensing inventory alone|DLP can identify sensitive data and enforce rules against unauthorized movement or disclosure.
2. Cloud Data Security|sanitize|A provider says deleting a file pointer permanently removes the underlying data. What is the best response?|Assess media sanitization and data remanence, including replicas and backups|Assume logical deletion always sanitizes every copy|Disable encryption to speed deletion|Accept a screenshot as proof that all media was destroyed|Logical deletion does not necessarily render stored data unrecoverable; sanitization requires appropriate methods and verification.
2. Cloud Data Security|sanitize|When is cryptographic erase a defensible sanitization approach?|When appropriate encryption protected the target data and all relevant decryption keys are securely eliminated|Whenever any one password is changed|Whenever a filename is renamed|Whenever a disk is disconnected temporarily|Cryptographic erase depends on suitable prior encryption and reliable elimination of keys capable of recovering the data.
2. Cloud Data Security|nist_privacy|A company moves data to a different cloud region. Which question matters most for data governance?|Which jurisdictions, access conditions, and obligations apply to all relevant copies and processing?|Whether the new region uses the same marketing slogan|Whether a different browser color theme is available|Whether all legal obligations automatically cease after migration|Location and cross-border processing can affect privacy, access, and legal obligations.
2. Cloud Data Security|logging|An investigation needs to prove how evidence changed hands. Which practice directly supports this?|Documented chain of custody with integrity verification|Copying files repeatedly without recording who handled them|Editing the original evidence to simplify analysis|Using only unsynchronized local timestamps|Chain of custody and integrity checks help demonstrate the provenance and handling of evidence.
2. Cloud Data Security|outline|A training dataset contains confidential customer records. What should precede using it in an external AI service?|Evaluate authorized use, privacy, minimization, and provider handling of the data|Upload it because AI training is always exempt from privacy duties|Remove all data classification labels|Assume model weights can never reveal training information|AI dataset use needs security and privacy review; training does not remove data-protection obligations.
3. Cloud Platform and Infrastructure Security|aws_shared|Which IaaS control is usually implemented by the cloud provider?|Physical protection and maintenance of its data-center hardware|The customer's application-level account approvals|The customer's guest OS configuration|The customer's database query authorization|The provider protects the underlying infrastructure; customer-controlled workload configuration remains a separate responsibility.
3. Cloud Platform and Infrastructure Security|zero|Why isolate management-plane access from ordinary application traffic?|A management-plane compromise can change or control cloud resources|Management APIs are harmless because they do not serve business pages|Isolation makes every administrative account unnecessary|Management traffic can never contain sensitive operations|Administrative interfaces deserve strong authentication, authorization, and constrained access because of their broad impact.
3. Cloud Platform and Infrastructure Security|zero|An attacker compromises one workload. Which control most directly limits movement to unrelated workloads?|Segmentation and narrowly allowed service communications|Granting all workloads the same administrator credential|Putting all services in one unrestricted security group|Disabling internal telemetry|Segmentation and least-privilege communications limit lateral movement and blast radius.
3. Cloud Platform and Infrastructure Security|bcdr|What does Recovery Time Objective describe?|The target maximum time to restore an interrupted service|The acceptable amount of lost data measured in time|The number of daily backups|The annual probability of a breach|RTO concerns restoration time. RPO concerns the point in time to which data must be recovered.
3. Cloud Platform and Infrastructure Security|bcdr|What does Recovery Point Objective describe?|The acceptable recovery point and associated amount of data loss measured in time|The maximum time to restore user access|The service's CPU utilization|The time required to rotate every certificate|RPO measures the tolerated data-loss interval relative to an incident.
3. Cloud Platform and Infrastructure Security|bcdr|A business tolerates 15 minutes of lost data. Which requirement should drive backup or replication design?|An RPO no greater than 15 minutes|An RTO of exactly 15 days|A log-retention period of 15 minutes|A patch cycle of 15 months|Recovery mechanisms should meet the tolerated data-loss interval; an RTO alone does not specify it.
3. Cloud Platform and Infrastructure Security|bcdr|A replicated database propagates an accidental deletion to its replica. What lesson follows?|Replication alone is insufficient protection against logical corruption or deletion|Replication guarantees recovery from every logical error|Backups are never needed if multiple regions exist|Only physical disk failures matter|A usable recovery strategy needs suitable recovery points and tested restoration, not only live replicas.
3. Cloud Platform and Infrastructure Security|bcdr|A provider advertises high availability. How should a customer validate its own disaster recovery readiness?|Test restoration and failover against business recovery requirements|Assume the provider's SLA proves application recovery|Count availability zones without testing|Check only that a backup job starts|Availability commitments do not prove that the customer's application can restore data and dependencies within its objectives.
3. Cloud Platform and Infrastructure Security|csa|What is a central security concern in multitenant infrastructure?|Maintaining isolation between tenants and workloads|Giving every tenant unrestricted shared storage access|Requiring all tenants to share one encryption key|Turning off management-plane authorization|Resource sharing requires controls that maintain logical separation and prevent unauthorized cross-tenant access.
3. Cloud Platform and Infrastructure Security|outline|An AI training cluster handles highly sensitive data. Which infrastructure design is strongest?|Isolated workloads, restricted administration, protected keys, and controlled data access|Unrestricted cluster-to-cluster access for performance|One shared administrator key in every training script|Public storage with an obscure object name|AI workloads require the same isolation and administration controls as other sensitive cloud workloads, adapted to their data and compute needs.
4. Cloud Application Security|threat|When should threat modeling first influence an application's design?|Early enough to shape requirements and architecture, then throughout changes|Only after a production breach|Only after all features are permanently frozen|Only during provider contract renewal|Threat modeling identifies assets, trust boundaries, threats, and mitigations before decisions become expensive to change.
4. Cloud Application Security|testing|Which activity is Static Application Security Testing?|Analyzing source or compiled code without executing the application|Sending attack requests to the running application|Manually approving a production deployment|Measuring the provider's HVAC temperature|SAST analyzes code artifacts; DAST examines behavior of a running application.
4. Cloud Application Security|testing|Which activity is Dynamic Application Security Testing?|Testing the running application through its exposed interfaces|Inspecting only source-code text|Checking only open-source licenses|Reviewing only the development team's access badges|DAST exercises a live application to identify vulnerabilities observable in its behavior.
4. Cloud Application Security|component|A build includes third-party libraries. Which practice directly evaluates their dependency risks?|Software composition analysis|Disk defragmentation|Renaming all package files|Turning off CI logging|SCA identifies third-party components and associated vulnerability or licensing risks.
4. Cloud Application Security|authorization|A user changes an object ID in an API request and reads another customer's record. Which missing control is most directly implicated?|Object-level authorization|Additional CSS validation|A longer hostname|A faster load balancer|The API must authorize the caller's access to the particular object, not merely authenticate the user.
4. Cloud Application Security|rest|An API accepts a token but does not verify its signature or applicable claims. What is the main concern?|Untrusted tokens could be accepted as proof of identity or access|The token is automatically converted into a secure session|The API becomes immune to replay|The API can no longer return JSON|Tokens must be validated according to the scheme, including signature and relevant issuer, audience, and expiry checks.
4. Cloud Application Security|authorization|Why is hiding an administrative button insufficient access control?|Authorization must be enforced on the protected server operation|Hidden buttons prevent all direct API requests|Client HTML always proves the user's permissions|The browser alone is a trusted policy decision point|Users can call APIs directly; permissions must be checked where resources and operations are protected.
4. Cloud Application Security|secrets|What is the best way for a workload to obtain cloud credentials where supported?|Use workload identity or short-lived, narrowly scoped credentials|Commit a long-lived administrator secret to source control|Include production keys in a public container image|Reuse a developer's personal password across every service|Workload identity and short-lived credentials reduce exposure and simplify revocation compared with embedded long-lived secrets.
4. Cloud Application Security|rest|What role does a web application firewall serve?|A supplementary layer for detecting or blocking certain malicious web requests|A replacement for all authorization and secure coding|A guarantee that dependencies contain no vulnerabilities|A replacement for customer identity management|A WAF can reduce some web attack exposure but does not remove application design and implementation responsibilities.
4. Cloud Application Security|authorization|An AI agent reads an untrusted document containing instructions to export secrets. Which architecture best limits impact?|Treat retrieved content as untrusted and enforce tool permissions outside the model|Give the model administrator rights so it can decide freely|Rely only on a hidden system prompt while granting broad tool access|Disable all auditing to reduce token use|Model output is not an authorization boundary. Independent access checks and constrained tools limit damage from manipulated instructions.
5. Cloud Security Operations|incident|What should an incident response plan establish before an incident?|Roles, escalation paths, evidence handling, and provider coordination|An assumption that the provider handles every customer incident|An instruction to delete all logs immediately|Only a list of product logos|Preparation and coordination make detection, response, and recovery more effective.
5. Cloud Security Operations|logging|Which logging approach best supports event correlation across cloud services?|Consistent timestamps and event identity with protected centralized collection|Each system using unrecorded local time without synchronization|Logging only successful page loads|Allowing any workload to alter all collected logs|Useful correlation requires consistent time and context; log integrity and restricted access preserve evidentiary value.
5. Cloud Security Operations|logging|Which information should generally be excluded or carefully redacted from ordinary application logs?|Passwords, access tokens, and unnecessary sensitive personal data|Event timestamps|Action names|Non-sensitive request correlation IDs|Logs are another data store and should not expose reusable credentials or unnecessary sensitive records.
5. Cloud Security Operations|incident|A compromised workload needs containment while forensic evidence is preserved. Which action is best?|Follow the response plan to isolate it and preserve relevant evidence before destructive changes|Immediately wipe it without recording any evidence|Publish all credentials to the incident ticket|Leave it unrestricted indefinitely to collect more data|Containment and evidence preservation must be coordinated according to the incident's impact and response procedures.
5. Cloud Security Operations|secrets|A service credential is exposed in a public repository. What is the priority response?|Revoke or rotate it and investigate its use and exposure|Delete the local file and assume the secret is now safe|Wait for the next annual audit|Change the repository's font size|Removal does not undo exposure. Revocation or rotation and investigation address the compromised credential.
5. Cloud Security Operations|csa|A deployment changes production permissions. What operational control is most relevant?|Reviewed change management with validation and a recovery plan|Allowing changes only when no one is watching|Disabling configuration history|Treating every emergency as an exception without documentation|Controlled changes improve traceability and reduce unintended production risk.
5. Cloud Security Operations|bcdr|What is the best evidence that backups meet recovery needs?|A successful restoration exercise checked against RTO and RPO|A screenshot showing that a backup job is scheduled|The storage provider's logo|A folder named latest-backup|Recoverability must be demonstrated by restoration, including dependencies and target recovery objectives.
5. Cloud Security Operations|csa|What is configuration drift?|A deployed environment diverging from its approved configuration baseline|An increase in encrypted file size|A change in the data controller's legal name only|A fixed mapping between two DNS records|Baseline comparison helps detect unexpected or unauthorized configuration changes.
5. Cloud Security Operations|incident|How do incident management and problem management differ conceptually?|Incident management restores service; problem management investigates underlying causes and recurrence|Problem management means closing every alert without investigation|Incident management is only a procurement activity|They always require identical actions and timelines|Restoring service and preventing recurrence are related but distinct operational objectives.
5. Cloud Security Operations|outline|An automated AI-assisted response system proposes disabling a business-critical account. What governance is appropriate?|Defined authorization limits, auditable actions, and human review where impact requires it|Trust all model outputs as approved changes|Remove rollback capability|Allow unbounded autonomous administrative actions|Automation needs accountable, risk-appropriate controls rather than implicit authority from a model recommendation.
6. Legal, Risk and Compliance|gdpr|Under the GDPR role definitions, who determines the purposes and means of personal-data processing?|Controller|Processor acting only on documented instructions|Storage hardware supplier automatically|Every employee as an independent controller|The controller decides why and how personal data is processed; a processor acts on behalf of the controller.
6. Legal, Risk and Compliance|gdpr|A cloud provider processes payroll data only on behalf of a customer according to instructions. Which role describes that activity?|Processor|Data subject|Independent controller for that activity automatically|Supervisory authority|A provider may act as a processor for the described activity. Actual roles depend on the specific processing, not only a contract label.
6. Legal, Risk and Compliance|nist_privacy|A cloud region is in one country, backups are elsewhere, and administrators operate globally. What must a privacy review consider?|Relevant processing locations, access paths, subprocessors, and cross-border obligations|Only the region's name in the marketing page|Only the customer's registered office|Only the app's display language|Privacy and legal risk analysis must consider actual processing and access, not just the primary storage region.
6. Legal, Risk and Compliance|outline|A dataset is subject to an active legal hold. What is the best retention decision?|Preserve the affected data under the hold and coordinate with authorized legal personnel|Automatically delete it when the ordinary retention period ends|Move it to another region to avoid the hold|Erase only backups without review|Legal holds can suspend routine deletion for affected information until the hold is properly released.
6. Legal, Risk and Compliance|assurance|A provider offers an audit report. What should a customer assess before relying on it?|Report scope, relevant services and regions, period, findings, and customer control responsibilities|Only whether the report uses the provider's current logo|Whether the report guarantees every customer's compliance|Whether the report eliminates the need for access reviews|Assurance evidence supports due diligence only when its coverage and limitations match the workload.
6. Legal, Risk and Compliance|nist_privacy|A vendor contract lacks data-export and deletion terms at termination. What risk should be addressed?|Exit capability and continued exposure of customer data|Only the application's color scheme|A guarantee of improved interoperability|The elimination of data ownership concerns|Cloud outsourcing requires defined arrangements for data access, return, and disposal, including termination.
6. Legal, Risk and Compliance|outline|A business formally accepts a documented residual risk within its approved appetite. Which treatment is this?|Risk acceptance|Risk avoidance|Risk transfer|Automatic risk elimination|Acceptance is an informed, authorized decision to retain risk; it does not claim the risk disappeared.
6. Legal, Risk and Compliance|nist_privacy|A company buys cyber insurance for a cloud workload. Which statement is most defensible?|Insurance may transfer some financial impact, while operational and compliance responsibilities remain|Insurance automatically prevents all security incidents|Insurance makes identity controls unnecessary|Insurance removes all data-protection duties|Transferring financial impact does not eliminate the underlying risk or the organization's other responsibilities.
6. Legal, Risk and Compliance|gdpr|A customer delegates processing to a cloud provider. What remains important for accountability?|Verify suitable safeguards and establish binding processing arrangements|Assume delegation eliminates the customer's duties|Treat encryption alone as proof of lawful processing|Rely solely on the provider's product name|The controller must ensure appropriate processing arrangements and safeguards; outsourcing does not by itself demonstrate compliance.
6. Legal, Risk and Compliance|outline|A company considers an AI service for sensitive decisions. What belongs in vendor due diligence?|Data handling, security controls, relevant obligations, accountability, and appropriate transparency|Only benchmark speed|Only the number of model parameters|Only the lowest subscription price|AI vendor assessment must consider security, privacy, legal, and governance implications alongside functionality.
